cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Internal-Inconsistency Checker: detecting multi-hop logical
  contradictions in documents via first-order logic and SMT solving
message: >-
  If you use this software or its ideas in academic work, please cite it
  using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Aria
    family-names: Mousavifar
    email: ariamousavifar@gmail.com
    affiliation: University of Genoa
    # orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000'   # add if you have one
repository-code: 'https://github.com/ariamousavifar/consistency-checker'
abstract: >-
  A pipeline that detects logical inconsistencies within a single document
  by extracting its claims, translating each into first-order logic, and
  discharging every logical judgement to the Z3 SMT solver. Unlike
  sentence-pair entailment approaches, it finds contradictions that no two
  sentences exhibit in isolation and that emerge only across a chain of
  inference. Where statements are mutually unsatisfiable it reports a
  minimal conflicting set together with a constructive derivation of the
  contradiction; where they are consistent it reconstructs the author's
  argument as a layered theory tree. A hybrid translation gate pairs a
  deterministic rule translator with a language model and admits a formula
  only when the two are proved equivalent or one passes a fidelity check,
  so no unverified translation reaches the solver. Content outside the
  supported logical fragment is quarantined with a recorded reason rather
  than silently discarded.
keywords:
  - natural language processing
  - first-order logic
  - automated reasoning
  - satisfiability modulo theories
  - semantic parsing
  - consistency checking
  - argument mining
  - large language models
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.8.8
date-released: '2026-07-03'
